\details{
Figure tags are placeholders written directly into manuscript text. They allow
users to refer to figures by filtering the figure metadata, rather than typing
figure numbers manually.

A figure tag has the form:

\verb{fg\{column = value\}}

where \code{column} is a column in the figure metadata and \code{value} is the value to
match.

For example:

\verb{fg\{taxon = Exemplomygale aculeatus\}}

will be replaced with a formatted figure reference for figures matching that
taxon.

More specific tags can be made by combining filters. Use:

\itemize{
\item \code{&} to require more than one condition to be matched
\item uppercase \code{OR} to allow more than one accepted value within a column
\item \verb{;} to separate alternative filter sets
}

For example:

\verb{fg\{taxon = Exemplomygale aculeatus & sex = male\}}

matches figures for male specimens of \verb{Exemplomygale aculeatus}.

This tag:

\verb{fg\{structure = fang OR chelicera & view = anterior\}}

matches figures where \code{structure} is either \code{fang} or \code{chelicera}, and \code{view}
is \code{anterior}.

This tag:

\verb{fg\{taxon = Exemplomygale aculeatus & sex = male; taxon = Exemplomygale eboracum & sex = female\}}

contains two alternative filter sets. It matches either male figures of
\verb{Exemplomygale aculeatus} or female figures of \verb{Exemplomygale eboracum}.

If a tag cannot be understood, or if it refers to columns that are not present
in \code{taxRmatic_figures}, the original tag is left unchanged and a warning is
issued. This makes problems easier to find without silently deleting text.
